    note: 'Nine currents, and one river beneath them. Syriac is the dialect of Aramaic that Edessa made into a literature, and its speakers lived at the crossing-place: between the Roman and Persian empires, then between Greek learning and Arabic, then between the church and the caliphate. What passed through their hands they translated, glossed and kept, so that the Syriac shelf holds not only its own mystics and apocalyptists but the sole surviving witnesses to a good deal of Greek thought. The technical vocabulary below is given unpointed, in the consonantal spelling of the manuscripts, since the eastern and western vocalisation systems differ; the transliteration beside each word follows the ordinary academic convention, and readers should expect the spellings of loanwords in particular to vary from codex to codex. These are the texts of churches that are still gathered and still praying, and the charm and amulet material is described as historians describe it, from the outside: no working formula is reproduced here, and nothing said of the past is meant as a description of anyone\'s practice now.',
